WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.460
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Shall we then move onto item
six on the agenda?

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.549
Jane, transforming care for people with a
learning disability and/or autism.

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:17.030
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thank you, Chair.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:23.460
This report just provides a progress update,
really, on what we have been doing.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.900
I just really wanted to start off by saying
that although the report does contain numbers

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.310
and plans, one of the things for me that is
really important is actually the impact we

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.040
are having on individual people's lives.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.980
One of the things we have done more recently
is spend time with people that have actually

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.282
been discharged, and their families.

00:00:43.282 --> 00:00:46.920
Some of the feedback we have had around the
difference it has made for individuals who

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.600
have been in the hospital, for example, over
20 years, that are actually now living in

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.700
their own homes, supported by carers and able
to choose and do what they want on a day-to-day

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.830
basis, really does bring it home to all of
us that work on this programme why we are

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:00.830
doing it.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.490
I think that is an important context to set.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:08.000
So overall, we are beginning to see I think
some progress.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
It is a very big and complex programme.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:17.320
It is not easy to do the things that we want
to do, but we have seen an overall 8.5% reduction

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:22.400
in the number of people that are inpatients.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.900
What we have also done over the last 12-15
months is had a real focus on identifying

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.670
the people that are actually in hospital.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:36.080
So what that has meant is that we have discovered
there were patients that were inpatients who

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.981
have been diagnosed with a learning disability
while an inpatient, or they have been in a

00:01:40.981 --> 00:01:43.650
mental health bed and not previously been
reported.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.850
So we have a significant focus now on actually
identifying those people who are in hospital

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:54.681
with a learning disability and/or autism alongside
a behaviour that challenges, which has meant

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:56.690
that they need to be in hospital.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.150
So if you looked at the total number of patients
we thought were in hospital in March last

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:06.580
year, we have actually seen a 30% reduction,
and I think that is quite significant.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:12.830
We have also seen over 190 people that have
been in hospital for over five years discharged.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.660
These people are often the ones with the most
complex needs.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.120
So taking time to make sure that, when they
are discharged, they are discharged appropriately

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.280
with the right support, has been really important.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.760
The other area that I think is worth identifying
is the progress we have made in terms of actual

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.870
bed reductions.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:37.331
Part of the plan we have is to reduce the
overall number of beds between 35% and 50%

00:02:37.331 --> 00:02:38.560
in three years.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.340
What we have started to see now are beds actually
closing, and the paper describes some of those.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.970
So we have had assessment and treatment units
that have been closed, we have had something

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:53.160
like 55 beds in total that have already stopped.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:03:00.630
We have also created, almost like forerunners
to STPs, transforming care partnerships.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:05.599
The purpose of that was to bring people together
so that they started to commission and work

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:10.180
on a wider footprint, alongside providers
and alongside people with a learning disability.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.090
Each of those TCPs have now submitted their
plans.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:20.990
The plans are highly ambitious, they are clearly
setting out a plan to deliver at the upper

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:27.620
end of the percentage that we were looking
for, and those have all been subject to quite

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.790
detailed confirm and challenge sessions from
the regions.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:38.459
So alongside that we have agreed that we are
going to do some additional work, almost like

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:42.830
a four point delivery plan: what are the things
that are going to have the biggest impact?

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.810
Because there is a lot of work to do and because
it is complicated, one of the things there

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:50.720
is a risk of is that we really do spread ourselves
too thinly.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.500
It is a point that had been made by Ian in
the past about, do you do something well but

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.489
at depth and then spread, or do you try and
do a lot of things on a shallow level?

00:03:59.489 --> 00:04:03.659
So what we are looking at doing at doing is
actually doing things well, understanding

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:04.920
that and then spreading it.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.569
So we are identifying high impact actions;
we are looking at we have called strategic

00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:16.150
re settlement, which is place based, population
based commissioning, on a big scale, looking

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:21.060
at what is needed for the people locally,
so we commission the right services in a community

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.060
setting.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:27.100
We are looking at extending the fast tracks,
which is what we put in last year, and really

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.389
concentrating time and effort where we are
going to have the biggest impact on people,

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:35.560
and also improving our data quality.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:41.740
So alongside that comes some additional funding,
and we'll shortly be announcing the additional

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.830
transformational funding that we are making
available to the transforming care partnerships,

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:50.210
and also the additional capital funding that
we have available for them.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.910
That announcement will be very, very soon.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:59.460
The other area I wanted to really focus on
and just flag is that we have talked a lot

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:06.070
about changing what happens at Calderstones
Foundation Trust, and alongside that was a

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.550
plan for Calderstones to be acquired by Mersey
Care.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.889
That has now happened, and it has happened
within the timescale that we agreed.

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:18.060
So Mersey Care became a foundation trust and
they have now acquired Calderstones Foundation

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.389
Trust, and the Calderstones name does not
exist.

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:22.389
It is now part of Mersey Care.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:30.180
We are hoping to shortly also launch the consultation
on the future use of that site.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:38.070
So I think in summary I would say that we
are beginning to make and have made some progress.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:43.070
It is a complex, difficult programme, it is
not something that NHS England can do on its

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:44.110
own.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.729
One of the benefits of this programme is that
we are working in partnership with ADASS,

00:05:48.729 --> 00:05:56.080
LGA, Association of Directors of Children's
Services, alongside NHS Improvement, HEE and

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.080
CQC.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.760
It really is a true partnership across all
those organisations.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:07.539
NHS England takes the lead and I chair the
delivery board, alongside Ray James from ADASS,

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:12.860
but I think it is important that we do recognise
this is a system response.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:21.600
It is almost in a bigger sense a societal
response to how we, as a society, treat and

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.070
care for and support people with a learning
disability to live a life that they want to

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.039
live, with the choices that they need and
want.

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:34.180
So I will not go into any more detail on that,
other than to say we have made progress, we

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.100
will continue to make progress and I am very
happy to take any questions.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:38.680
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Jane.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.350
The board has taken a very strong interest
in this from the beginning, so commendation

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.570
to you and the team for the progress that
has been made.

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:47.570
Yes, please, Victor.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:48.570
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Well done, Jane.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.270
I think it is a really clear exposition.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.620
We have been around the circle, a clear plan.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:07:01.960
I just want to test an assumption that the
change in the name regarding Calderstones

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.840
is also a change in function.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.900
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Not yet.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:14.100
We are decommissioning beds at the moment,
and have done, from Calderstones, or from

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.460
that Whalley site.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:21.120
The consultation which is just being finalised
will actually set out what the plans are for

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.466
that site, and that is part of building the
right support, and it is part of our plan

00:07:26.466 --> 00:07:30.789
to commission services around the needs of
people in that part of the world.

00:07:30.789 --> 00:07:35.660
The other issue is that there are several
people in Calderstones, on that Whalley site,

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.229
that actually are from around the country.

00:07:37.229 --> 00:07:41.800
So there is something about repatriating people
to close to where they come from.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:45.970
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: Can you just say as much
as you can say about what the current barriers

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.889
are to making faster progress?

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:52.669
Because you have clearly made progress, but
you would like to move faster.

00:07:52.669 --> 00:07:56.080
So I am just wondering whether you can say
anything about the barriers?

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:02.860
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Some of the barriers are
around determining what is the right service

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.650
to commission in the community.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:10.759
The fast tracks we have had running so far
have often commissioned crisis response teams,

00:08:10.759 --> 00:08:18.210
providing support seven days a week, being
able to identify the 24,000-25,000 of the

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:24.520
population that are most at risk of admission,
so that the care is almost pre emptive rather

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.410
than crisis.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.410
So that is some of the work we have done.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:33.380
The other big issue, I think it is fair to
say, is that because for an individual CCG

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:38.019
the numbers of people that they might need
to commission services for are quite small,

00:08:38.019 --> 00:08:44.269
there is a lack of expertise in terms of being
able to make sure that the focus is on those

00:08:44.269 --> 00:08:45.269
individuals.

00:08:45.269 --> 00:08:50.559
So by bringing people together in the transforming
care partnerships, we are bringing that expertise

00:08:50.559 --> 00:08:51.889
together for a wider footprint.

00:08:51.889 --> 00:08:57.470
So it is back to the place based type commissioning
that we need to do.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:04.269
Then another key one is that in some cases
it is quite difficult to recruit the right

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:09.279
people to be able to support care, and we
have to double run for some of these services,

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:14.089
which is why we are giving transformation
funding out so that we can actually double

00:09:14.089 --> 00:09:19.800
run, we can provide the funding to move with
people when they come out of hospital into

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.100
a community setting.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.769
That can be quite complex to do when you are
doing it for individuals.

00:09:25.769 --> 00:09:26.790
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: John?

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:33.689
&gt;&gt;John Burn: Jane, you referred there to the
small group of target population in each CCG.

00:09:33.689 --> 00:09:38.110
In the definition of the paper we used the
term 'autism', but of course clinically we

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:43.009
have now moved towards 'autistic spectrum
disorder', and that is really a gradation

00:09:43.009 --> 00:09:45.399
that blends into normal society.

00:09:45.399 --> 00:09:50.749
So it brings with it the danger of mission
creep; that you end up with an ever broader

00:09:50.749 --> 00:09:53.110
population that would fall under this remit.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:57.350
Can you just reassure us that we have a clear
idea of who our target population are?

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:01.699
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Yes, I think that is a really
good point.

00:10:01.699 --> 00:10:07.259
So obviously, some individuals with autism
or autistic spectrum disorder also have a

00:10:07.259 --> 00:10:08.259
learning disability.

00:10:08.259 --> 00:10:10.790
Others may have behaviour that challenges.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:16.579
So where we are concentrating, and the definition
we used in building the right support, was

00:10:16.579 --> 00:10:19.959
people with a learning disability and/or autism
with behaviour that challenges.

00:10:19.959 --> 00:10:26.860
I do not like the term 'challenging behaviour',
because it feels quite negative, but the impact

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:31.819
that that might have on individuals and their
families and the ability to live in the community

00:10:31.819 --> 00:10:35.290
setting, is often the issue that sparks an
admission.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.139
That is what we are addressing.

00:10:38.139 --> 00:10:40.019
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Moira?

00:10:40.019 --> 00:10:42.529
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thanks, Malcolm.

00:10:42.529 --> 00:10:50.799
Jane, this is encouraging and I am sure this
is incredibly complex to manage.

00:10:50.799 --> 00:11:00.110
I just wanted to ask, because you are taking
a person focused, individual focused approach,

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:07.339
and finding alternative and better placements
for individuals: the decommissioning of placements,

00:11:07.339 --> 00:11:13.860
as it were, could therefore be seen to be
random rather than planned.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:20.470
Is there a risk that we end up decommissioning
a significant amount of NHS provision, and

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:29.420
actually later on when placements might break
down or not be suitable any longer, we are

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:34.410
reliant on private sector alternatives?

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:41.339
So you are planning for individuals, but actually
what would you plan for a population?

00:11:41.339 --> 00:11:44.923
Will we end up in the right place with the
population plan as well as the individuals?

00:11:44.923 --> 00:11:51.249
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: So I think the key answer
to that is, in the words of Helen Bevan, it

00:11:51.249 --> 00:11:52.249
is 'both and.'

00:11:52.249 --> 00:11:57.420
You have to look at individuals, but part
of the approach around strategic resettlement

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:02.100
is that we look at a bigger place based idea.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.319
So you look at the needs of that particular
population, in terms of numbers of people

00:12:05.319 --> 00:12:09.639
that might be at risk of admission and what
type of services they might need collectively.

00:12:09.639 --> 00:12:12.759
So you have to do this on an individual basis.

00:12:12.759 --> 00:12:16.370
However, if we do that, we miss the point
about what the wider needs are.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:20.819
There are some parts of the country where
the number of inpatient beds available are

00:12:20.819 --> 00:12:26.579
actually quite low, and in many cases we might
need to commission more in those parts of

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:28.420
the world.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:33.800
Alongside the 'how many do we need?', we need
to think through the totality of the beds,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.449
not just one form or another.

00:12:36.449 --> 00:12:42.019
We are looking at the whole inpatient provision,
not just those that are NHS provided.

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:46.339
So it is a good point, and it is something
that is part of the TCP plans.

00:12:46.339 --> 00:12:51.569
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Jane, thank you very much
for the paper and for the continuing good

00:12:51.569 --> 00:12:53.370
work on this.

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:58.519
I think I just need to invite the board to
note the paper, to note the review timetable

00:12:58.519 --> 00:13:05.619
you have for the TCPs, and also the approval
to endorse the four point delivery plan in

00:13:05.619 --> 00:13:06.619
appendix A.

00:13:06.619 --> 00:13:08.600
We are content to do all of those things.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.059
Thank you very much.

